direction: right

title: |md
  ## DSQ patterns: shared vs per-CPU dispatch
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

global: "Global DSQ\n(SCX_DSQ_GLOBAL)" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  g_body: |md
    Single queue, all CPUs pull from it.

    Pros:
    - Simple
    - Automatic load balancing
      (idle CPUs grab next task)

    Cons:
    - Cache locality lost
    - Lock contention at high rate
    - No latency guarantees

    Used by: scx_simple (default)
  |
}

percpu: "Per-CPU DSQ\n(scx_bpf_create_dsq)" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  p_body: |md
    Each CPU has its own queue.

    Pros:
    - Cache locality
    - No lock contention
    - Per-CPU policy possible

    Cons:
    - Manual load balancing in BPF
    - Idle CPUs starve if no work locally

    Used by: scx_central, scx_flatcg
  |
}

central: "Central DSQ\n(scx_central pattern)" {
  style.fill: "#dbeafe"
  ce_body: |md
    One CPU acts as 'central'
    dispatcher; others enqueue
    but never dispatch directly.

    Pros:
    - Centralized policy decisions
    - Flexible (anti-thrashing,
      latency-priority queues, ...)

    Cons:
    - Central CPU is bottleneck
    - Cross-CPU IPI cost

    Used by: scx_central
  |
}

global -> central: "scaling up" {style.stroke-dash: 4}
percpu -> central: "with central control" {style.stroke-dash: 4}
